#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "31a9b20e-d159-5ff2-94dc-54b8d7b8e620" :question/math-academy-id "e-21785" :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3,$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} +  f_{2}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = y,  f_2 = 0,  f_1 = -1,$ and $f_0 = y^2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y = e^{3x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{3x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})+{({e}^{3x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & =27{e}^{6x}-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}({e}^{6x}+27{e}^{3x}-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078256 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec3823db-6df8-502a-a050-b5aa169d2e9d" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Constant Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "91e7f8e9-dc03-5203-9d33-0175960ba276" :question/math-academy-id "e-6570" :question/problem "Given that $D=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+3 \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}+5,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=e^{- x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = a_{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + a_{1}\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + a_0y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  $a_2 = 1, a_1 = 3,$ and $a_0 = 5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+5){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})+3\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078257 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ff7f935-dcec-5b4c-ba1d-e0b4c9e197a4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Functions of X as Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "0fba12b1-fa78-5ff2-88e4-2a690105d321" :question/math-academy-id "e-6571" :question/problem "Given that $D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}}{\\textrm d x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-5x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7066d712-f0a3-554f-9d10-3d6fe4b95a18" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Differential Operators With Multivariable Functions as Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5936ad5d-47c1-54c8-b496-760a09fa8130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(6-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297574} {:answer/id #uuid "c93b093d-82c3-5b47-8a9a-c34b0321dc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297575} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8641b5-6ade-5521-b3db-ca0bab88c272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297576} {:answer/id #uuid "a238c216-92e1-53f5-b4d6-87d88a93a1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297577} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0584f1-0aae-50ac-b6e5-640ece58a243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a238c216-92e1-53f5-b4d6-87d88a93a1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297577} :answer-field/id #uuid "bde7448d-6255-54c0-936b-fe929ae4a8c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9314e42-73f2-5134-b5e1-c3785c0ac3fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-338254" :question/problem "If $D(y)=xy\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+3y,$ then $D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x,y)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}=xy,{f}_{2}=0,{f}_{1}=0,$ and ${f}_{0}=3.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(3-8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
